845
01:10:43,040 --> 01:10:46,721
The requested amount of 3,500 is the same amount that was budgeted as last year.

846
01:10:47,662 --> 01:10:57,006
Next line down, professional services is for services performed by KP Law, which covers the monthly fee along with court and associated labor costs and the like.

847
01:10:57,767 --> 01:11:00,668
The proposed amount of 8,000, excuse me, 81,600 is the same as last year.

848
01:11:04,026 --> 01:11:11,489
The professional services account, which used to be called the witness and entry fee, pays for constable costs and other costs associated with litigation.

849
01:11:11,509 --> 01:11:15,651
The request remains the same in previous years, which is $1,100.

850
01:11:16,712 --> 01:11:22,794
The printing of briefs account pays for transcripts of depositions and the like, remains the same as last year of $1,600.

851
01:11:23,014 --> 01:11:30,477
The office supplies account, next line down, pays for pens, paper, et cetera, and remains the same request as last year of $1,500.

852
01:11:33,571 --> 01:11:39,754
It goes on to the publications account, which pays for updated Westlaw materials with respect to law books, guides, and the like.

853
01:11:40,154 --> 01:11:43,676
Use of Lexis Advance, which is a software tool available for attorneys.

854
01:11:44,716 --> 01:11:49,579
The other expenditures account, we typically use to pay for computer issues or Janice's typewriter.

855
01:11:49,619 --> 01:11:51,800
Yes, she still uses a typewriter.

856
01:11:51,900 --> 01:11:54,861
And the requested amount is $1,500, which remains the same.

857
01:11:56,322 --> 01:11:58,303
And we come to the claims over and under accounts now.

858
01:12:00,068 --> 01:12:07,896
They're listed as claims under $1,000 and claims over $1,000, but are really claims over and under $2,500.

859
01:12:10,418 --> 01:12:20,208
Now for the newer counselors, typically for claims that are filed within my office, they usually fall under two laws, namely the

860
01:12:21,783 --> 01:12:23,424
It's called the Street Defect Statute.

861
01:12:23,544 --> 01:12:37,454
Some call it the Pothole Law, and it's found within Chapter 84 of the Massachusetts General Laws, which makes the city liable for personal injury or property damage caused by a defective condition on our public ways or sidewalks.

862
01:12:38,415 --> 01:12:48,162
Chapter 258 is the second one, which is also known as the Massachusetts Store Claims Act, which makes the city liable for personal injury and property damage caused by the negligence of its employees.

863
01:12:49,433 --> 01:12:54,396
Now, each statute has its own provisions and limitations to the extent which the city may be held liable.

864
01:12:55,117 --> 01:12:58,499
Under Chapter 84, the liability limit is $5,000.

865
01:12:58,879 --> 01:13:03,062
Under Chapter 258, the liability limit is $100,000 per claimant.

866
01:13:04,103 --> 01:13:11,067
Other claims such as zoning appeals are litigated in land court and licensing appeals, which are typically appealed to Somerville District Court.

867
01:13:11,928 --> 01:13:17,392
Now, throughout this past fiscal year, my office has received 103 claims, which are broken up as follows.

868
01:13:18,594 --> 01:13:35,019
36 claims were received under Chapter 258 involving property damage, three claims were filed under Chapter 258 involving personal injuries, 36 claims were filed under Chapter 84 involving property damage, and five involving personal injuries under Chapter 84.

869
01:13:35,759 --> 01:13:40,260
The remaining 23 claims involve licensing appeals, zoning appeals, personnel matters, and the like.

870
01:13:43,173 --> 01:13:54,135
And as records access officer for the city of Method, I have received approximately 100 public record requests within the past fiscal year to which I have responded to pursuant to the requirements of the Massachusetts Public Records Law.

871
01:13:55,375 --> 01:13:56,556
And with that, I'll take any questions.

872
01:13:56,876 --> 01:13:57,176
Thank you.

873
01:13:57,436 --> 01:13:58,396
Thanks, Senator Scanlon.

