1252
01:44:01,522 --> 01:44:10,270
Many of our employees are making the state minimum wage or making just a dollar or two above that.

1253
01:44:11,151 --> 01:44:15,113
Every time the state minimum wage is going up, of course, you'll see an increase to that.

1254
01:44:16,213 --> 01:44:33,220
And there's also an increase with our lifeguarding staff, again, a very slight amount, but trying to be as competitive as the budget allows to be able to make sure that we can maintain Wrights Pond and Tufts Pool this summer with adequate lifeguard staff.

1255
01:44:35,000 --> 01:44:36,321
And that's about all the questions.

1256
01:44:37,061 --> 01:44:39,002
We have Councilor Scarpelli and then Vice President Behrs.

1257
01:44:44,594 --> 01:44:57,778
I want to make sure people understand that during the pandemic, you had police, fire, DPW, other major departments working throughout the pandemic.

1258
01:44:59,238 --> 01:45:02,239
And Kevin, I believe recreation was also there, correct?

1259
01:45:03,980 --> 01:45:05,060
I'm sorry, I missed that last part.

1260
01:45:05,720 --> 01:45:10,101
I believe the recreation department was there through the pandemic to assist in any issue.

1261
01:45:12,404 --> 01:45:12,704
Absolutely.

1262
01:45:12,724 --> 01:45:19,847
During the pandemic itself we did work with the YMCA with the summer with with the park program to distribute food.

1263
01:45:20,707 --> 01:45:23,888
When everything was shut down we did household deliveries.

1264
01:45:26,808 --> 01:45:29,509
all of the food programs at the different parks.

1265
01:45:30,310 --> 01:45:41,694
When you look at coming out and being able to open up facilities, you know, MedFed, Somerville, there's very few departments that actually kept their pond and their pool open.

1266
01:45:41,914 --> 01:45:47,577
And we actually had a recreation working group among Massachusetts, but particularly along this area.

1267
01:45:48,337 --> 01:45:50,538
And there was actually an aquatic one specific

1268
01:45:51,028 --> 01:45:54,310
that myself and several other recreation directors were a part of.

1269
01:45:54,771 --> 01:46:02,856
And our goal is trying to figure out how to navigate through the COVID-19 restrictions and doing daily fees and trying to do some type of cost recovery.

1270
01:46:03,817 --> 01:46:10,741
So fortunately, we were one of the few facilities that kept our pool and our pond open during COVID-19.

1271
01:46:11,322 --> 01:46:13,984
Well, I say this because I wanna make a point.

1272
01:46:14,224 --> 01:46:18,527
Obviously, those people who know me know my background as in recreation, those people who don't know me,

1273
01:46:19,283 --> 01:46:31,128
I was given the honor of chairing the committee that made our recreation department a full-time year-round program, something that I find very, very important.

1274
01:46:32,589 --> 01:46:42,333
I believe that a recreation department in a community, a full-time recreation program is a natural municipal necessity.

1275
01:46:49,806 --> 01:47:03,709
It's especially during the pandemic when we saw all of our children and seniors and adults looking at some very difficult times.

1276
01:47:04,449 --> 01:47:07,930
And the recreation department was there to give them some sort of normalcy.

1277
01:47:08,570 --> 01:47:27,747
so i i commend you and your staff as minimal as they are um people don't understand i have as a recreation a program developer which is also an assistant director we have a director a facilities operation manager a full-time administrative assistant i have six full-time program coordinators

1278
01:47:28,728 --> 01:47:31,589
I have over 100 part-time staff.

1279
01:47:32,049 --> 01:47:41,332
And then in the summer, I'm given 120 mere summer jobs students, 14 to 18 years old, that work for Recreation Department.

1280
01:47:42,152 --> 01:47:44,993
So I understand the limitations you're working with.

1281
01:47:45,113 --> 01:47:47,653
And you're doing so much, Kevin, with so little.

1282
01:47:47,753 --> 01:47:48,594
And I applaud you.

1283
01:47:49,374 --> 01:47:52,775
Because as a self-sufficient program, a self-funding program,

1284
01:47:53,516 --> 01:47:55,080
It's very difficult to achieve that.

1285
01:47:55,401 --> 01:47:59,854
And the way I was always raised in recreation is

1286
01:48:01,472 --> 01:48:10,415
When it comes to making money off of kids, it's difficult to take, and we do the best we can, but more importantly, it's what we offer.

1287
01:48:10,955 --> 01:48:29,701
When you talked about the teen program on Friday nights, when you're talking about servicing 100 Medford teens on a Friday evening, after the pandemic, looking to some sort of social connection to help with the mental state of mind, I thought that was amazing and something we should applaud.

1288
01:48:30,912 --> 01:48:35,633
But as we move forward, you said some things that made me a little nervous.

1289
01:48:37,153 --> 01:48:43,774
We know that the leadership staff at pools for the DCR are making between 25 and 26.

1290
01:48:45,115 --> 01:48:46,835
Lifeguards are making between 20 and 21.

1291
01:48:48,555 --> 01:48:57,997
I know in Somerville, what we've decided to do because of competition in neighboring communities and the lack of lifeguards, we had to increase our lifeguards fee to $21.

1292
01:49:00,355 --> 01:49:27,290
I'm afraid two of the strongest well-used programs during the summer, especially the pool and the pond, that if we, from what I gather, minimum wage, I hope, because we ask for assistance from the city to make sure that the fees are going to be, the pay structure is gonna be comparable or else we're going to lose lifeguards.

1293
01:49:28,310 --> 01:49:31,533
I know people don't think so, but I'll be honest with you.

1294
01:49:32,514 --> 01:49:47,446
If my daughter's friends are working in Medford and they're making $18 an hour, and some of them who's still looking to hire can hire at $21 an hour, I'll tell you what, we're going to lose kids quickly.

1295
01:49:47,807 --> 01:49:53,832
And what then happens is, with the lack of lifeguards, means the lack of hours of shutdown and

1296
01:49:55,625 --> 01:49:56,665
the pool and the pond.

1297
01:49:57,105 --> 01:49:58,566
I can only imagine that's what it'd be.

1298
01:49:58,606 --> 01:49:59,966
Can you correct me if I'm wrong, Kevin?

1299
01:50:03,387 --> 01:50:09,468
I think if anyone reads the Boston Globe, there's multiple articles out there about lifeguard shortages.

1300
01:50:09,548 --> 01:50:14,110
I think even publicly, people know that it is a struggle.

1301
01:50:14,130 --> 01:50:18,071
It is very competitive to get enough lifeguards.

1302
01:50:18,951 --> 01:50:23,932
A lot of people think that we start getting the pond and pool ready around April, May, June.

1303
01:50:24,366 --> 01:50:33,131
That's really not the case we started in December, we start December running lifeguard classes and starting on the school vacation weeks and we try to do a lot of recruitment.

1304
01:50:33,911 --> 01:50:43,417
It is a very, very competitive environment to try to get adequate lifeguards DCR has really changed the aquatic community and it is a big talk.

1305
01:50:44,629 --> 01:51:02,058
different aquatic professionals, not just in recreation, but YMCAs and all across anyone that is an aquatic director and friends and colleagues of mine have discussed the DCR right now is paying head lifeguards 21, sorry, lifeguards $21 an hour, head lifeguards $22 an hour.

1306
01:51:02,699 --> 01:51:12,584
They teach swim lessons, they get an additional $2 an hour and they're giving them a $500 sign on bonus and a $500 bonus if they stay until labor day.

1307
01:51:13,620 --> 01:51:16,102
On top of that, they also offer free lifeguard classes.

1308
01:51:17,222 --> 01:51:24,067
When I was first hired, I started offering lifeguard classes for $50 just to cover the expense that the Red Cross charges.

1309
01:51:25,268 --> 01:51:28,210
And that was relatively new.

1310
01:51:28,310 --> 01:51:30,831
It was a recruitment strategy that not everybody had.

1311
01:51:31,712 --> 01:51:32,693
It's the standard now.

1312
01:51:34,874 --> 01:51:36,956
It is important to get the right lifeguard.

1313
01:51:36,976 --> 01:51:38,276
You have to be able to offer that.

1314
01:51:38,397 --> 01:51:40,478
And Councilor Scarpelli, I think to your point,

1315
01:51:41,131 --> 01:51:46,574
It is true that we are trying to be as competitive as possible with that slight budget increase.

1316
01:51:47,755 --> 01:51:54,359
But there are going to be other places that are competitive and that are gonna be able to offer more than what the city is offering right now.

1317
01:51:55,139 --> 01:52:00,022
With that said, we have done a lot of work with recruitment.

1318
01:52:00,483 --> 01:52:05,826
And right now we actually have more lifeguards working this summer than we have in years past.

1319
01:52:06,226 --> 01:52:07,627
So we have done the recruitment.

1320
01:52:08,181 --> 01:52:13,503
We have tried to create an environment that is fun, that again is local, being a part of the community.

1321
01:52:15,444 --> 01:52:19,506
You can go lifeguard for DCR, but you're gonna be going to a different city or town.

1322
01:52:19,926 --> 01:52:21,467
You're not gonna be working with the community.

1323
01:52:21,747 --> 01:52:24,989
You're not gonna be in the facility that you grew up in.

1324
01:52:25,109 --> 01:52:27,830
And we try to really hone that point down to our staff.

1325
01:52:28,370 --> 01:52:34,053
And hopefully that will be another part to it that is important to make sure that we maintain operations.

1326
01:52:34,759 --> 01:52:44,567
The other part to your point about having to close if we don't have enough lifeguards, that is stuff that we have always planned for, not just here, but in previous jobs that I've worked in.

1327
01:52:44,987 --> 01:52:54,775
We make sure that all of our management team is also lifeguard certified so that if we do run into those situations, we do have staff that is able to jump in.

1328
01:52:55,936 --> 01:53:01,501
It is not uncommon, at least a couple of shifts during the summertime that you will see me on the guard chair as well.

1329
01:53:02,181 --> 01:53:04,103
And we try and make sure that we keep it open.

1330
01:53:04,783 --> 01:53:07,205
Okay, so what what is the fee for our per hour.

1331
01:53:08,046 --> 01:53:08,626
What are we paying.

1332
01:53:09,627 --> 01:53:24,441
What are we paying right now with the budget, we are at $18 an hour during the weekdays and we do an extra dollar $19 a weekend to try to incentivize which has actually helped to just try to give that extra dollar bump to work on the weekends

1333
01:53:25,562 --> 01:53:28,003
I'm glad to see that working.

1334
01:53:28,023 --> 01:53:30,124
So I, that's one of my concerns.

1335
01:53:30,164 --> 01:53:33,105
So I'm glad to hear that we, we at least stayed competitive.

1336
01:53:33,125 --> 01:53:33,665
So thank you.

1337
01:53:34,186 --> 01:53:39,288
Um, I know that I don't want to, there's a few other things that I want to make sure that we talk about that.

1338
01:53:39,808 --> 01:53:46,931
I know that working in summer, we'll get a phone calls for drink time, um, during the summer ice rink time.

1339
01:53:47,631 --> 01:53:48,312
And, um,

1340
01:53:49,292 --> 01:54:00,936
I had to explain to people that we gave the rink back to the state because of its huge financial impact to that city.

1341
